==Icelandic==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|non|is}} {{m|non|sól}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|is}} {{m|gem-pro|*sōwulō}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|is}} {{m|ine-pro|*sóh₂wl̥}}. Cognate with Old Church Slavonic {{m|cu|sc=Cyrl|слъньцє|tr=slŭnĭce}}, Latin {{m|la|sōl}}, Ancient Greek {{m|grc|ἥλιος}}, Sanskrit {{m|sa|sc=Deva|सूर|tr=sūra}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/souːl/|lang=is}} ({{audio|Is-sól.oga|listen|lang=is}})
*: {{rhymes|ouːl|lang=is}}

===Noun===
{{is-noun|f|sólar|sólir}}

# the [[sun]] {{gloss|star which illuminates one side of the Earth}}
#* ''Á Sprengisandi'' (“On Sprengisandur”) by [[w:en:Grímur Thomsen|Grímur Thomsen]]
#*: Ríðum, ríðum og rekum yfir sandinn,
#*: rennur '''sól''' á bak við Arnarfell,
#*: hér á reiki er margur óhreinn andinn,
#*: úr því fer að skyggja á jökulsvell;
#*: Drottinn leiði drösulinn minn,
#*: drjúgur verður síðasti áfanginn.
#*:: Ride, ride, ride hard across the sands,
#*:: the '''sun''' is settling behind Arnarfell.
#*:: Here many spirits of the dark
#*:: threaten in the gloom over the glacier's ice.
#*:: The Lord leads my horse,
#*:: it is still a long, long way home.
#: '''''Sólin''' er [[björt]].''
#:: The '''sun''' is [[bright]].
# [[sun]] {{gloss|applied to any star or used metaphorically}}

